---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
---

{/*
  A 404 is the one page whose depth is not known when it is written.

  Every asset reference on this site is relative (gate S2), computed from the page's
  own depth — and this page is built at depth 0. But a static host serves the
  *contents* of 404.html while the browser keeps the *requested* address, so a miss
  at `/unit-1/nope` resolves `assets/site.css` to `/unit-1/assets/site.css` and gets
  nothing. The page then renders completely unstyled, which reads as a broken site
  rather than a missing page — and that is how this was found: a dev-server URL
  returned the 404, and the unstyled 404 looked like the real page had lost its CSS.

  `repairBase` sets a `<base>` from the address bar before the stylesheet is parsed.
  Root-absolute paths would fix it too, and are exactly what S2 forbids because they
  break a subpath deploy.

  With JavaScript off, a miss below the root is unstyled but complete — the text and
  the link are both in the HTML. Accepted: a stylesheet cannot resolve against an
  unknown depth without either a script or an absolute path.
*/}
<Page title="Not found" depth={0} repairBase>
  <h1>That page isn't here</h1>
  <p class="lede">The link may be out of date, or the address may have a typo in it.</p>
  <p><a class="btn btn--primary" href="index.html">Go to the syllabus →</a></p>
</Page>
